A||N||9||87ECO5||Any Lawn Care Experts?||||5||
Z||000000||87ECO5||11-05-2000||11:37 AM||||It seems to me that this job is tougher than keeping my Fiero nice.  When do you water - early morning or late evening?  When do you use weed killer - spring, summer, winter, fall?  What kind do you use?  How about fertilizer?  Is it better to keep the grass long or short?  Is it better to bag or mulch?  When do you airate(sp?)?  What about bare spots?  Is it better to seed or sod?  What's the best way to get rid of tree roots that have surfaced and are now a burden on my mower's blade?<P>Are there websites out there that have all of the answers?<P>TIA||||reg||5
Z||000001||onfire||11-05-2000||01:45 PM||||Well my dad used to make me mow the lawn everyweek. One time he had me and my brother mow it the same day. So it looked like a checker board. Ahh the good 'ol days.<P>Anyway keep the grass long it takes less water. He always watered the grass in the evening. Used "Miracle Gro" on trees and flowers.If I remember anything else or talk to him I will post again.||||reg||1
Z||000002||onfire||11-05-2000||01:48 PM||||Oh yeah we had a mulch mower. As for weeds if its not to bad some kids pulling weeds always work. <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://homestead.deja.com/user.on_fire/files/onfirepff.JPG">   <BR>  <A HREF="http://homestead.deja.com/user.on_fire/on_fire.html" TARGET=_blank><B>Check out my little waist of bandwidth onfire</B></A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||11-05-2000||06:14 PM||||Grass is better off longer. Water in the evening, water gets to soak into the groung rather than burn off as the sun rises in the morning. Aerate in the spring, we rent one and pull it behind the lawnmower. Weed killer in the fall. We have one of the local lawn-care companies spray every fall and spring. We always seed and use straw to cover it up, so birds don't grab the seed. I just run the mover over the root repeatedly until the blade doesn't hit it anymore <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">, but I don't recommend that, but it can be quite fun <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Black88GT||11-05-2000||08:11 PM||||I work for Franks Nursery here in Baltimore, so I have some background here.<P>I think fiero56 beat me to it though with most of them.<P>Bare spots are most likely bad soil condition.  For example, if you have dogs, wherever they frequently piss will have a bare spot because there is too much acid in the soil.  The Ph is off in the soil so nothing is going to grow.  You can check this buy getting a Ph kit from any nursery store or Lowe's whatever.  If it has too much acid you need to use Lime to get it straight.  The ideal Ph would be 7, 14 is the highest (alkaline) 1 is the lowest (acid).  Golf courses test Ph levels daily.  If it is higher on the Ph scale you need to use Sulfur.  It will say how much to use on the bag.  <P>About Sod, sod has many benefits over seeding.  For one its instant gratification.  Secondly, since its already growing, its not as likely to dry out.  Its also easier to put sod on hills then seeding them since seeds will wash away.  Also, you can sod anytime of the year as long as the soil is not frozen.<P>However, buying sod is tough since there are so many factors that can make it good or bad.  With Sod you need to have in your posession ASAP so it doesn't die.  Some retailers will sell week old sod that will die a couple days after you plant it.<BR>The best way to do it is see if anyone in your area will ship it to you right after they cut it.<P>Seeds are good too because its a lot cheaper then sodding.  You need to water the lawn a lot and do whatever you can to keep the birds away (I find M-80s work well).  Also if you live in an area with a lot of rain you really don't have to do much but spread it out well.  <P>I would recommend Lawn Care for Dummies.  It's a great beginners book.<P>Its better to cut grass when its short and cut it often since fresh cut clippings act as a natural fertilizer (nitrogen)<P>Well I don't want to go on and on , so just buy the book and it will answer all your questions and more.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||6
Z||000005||Shiner||11-06-2000||03:38 AM||||Well, right now I am a landscaper/lawnboy part time.  Here is what I know about it.<P>1) You have to say "fu*%in' b*tch " a lot.<P>2) Always have coffee<P>3) Talk about needing to "use the crapper" at<BR>   least half a dozen times during one day.<P>4) Point out every attractive woman to your <BR>   collegues and wait for inapropriate <BR>   sexual inuendo.<P>5) If you don't have anything to do, open the tool box, take out a tool and look at it, sometimes setting it down on the truck and getting out another tool.<P> That's pretty much it.  I guess it doesn't solve your problems, but at least it will get you in the right mindset   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>  <p>[This message has been edited by Shiner (edited 11-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||87ECO5||11-06-2000||08:10 AM||||Thanks for the input, folks.  I have another question:<P>My yard is probably 75% weeds and 25% grass (I'm not kidding!).  With it being that bad, can it be brought back to life with weed killer, aeration, fertilizer, etc., or will it require starting over (stripping the bad turf and either re-seeding or re-sodding)?||||reg||5
Z||000007||fiero56||11-06-2000||08:30 AM||||Hey Scott,<P>Next time your over, ask my neighbor. He is "the gardening expert". Everytime he tries to cut his grass, every person in the neighborhood stops him, asking for advice. Takes him 6 hours instead of 2 to cut the grass because of it, I just stand in the driveway and laugh.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Black88GT||11-07-2000||03:12 PM||||I would just start over if it is that bad...<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR> <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||11-07-2000||06:44 PM||||Scott--Just concrete over the whole thing.  No muss, no fuss.  You'll never have to worry about weeds again.  (BTW--I suggested this to Anita last spring.  The idea didn't go over very well.)  BTW (pt 2)--You might want to consider pulling up your "deck" and laying some sand under the bricks.  It'll even out the bricks and might keep some of the weeds out, too.||||reg||1
